apshot bytes."," - 2026-10-10 — refresh under approval. Copied: low-level-requirements.toml (ref: WI-880); test-cases.toml (ref: WI-880). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."," - 2026-10-10 — refresh under approval. Copied: system-requirements.toml (re-attested: SR-019, SR-020, SR-032, SR-046). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes.","+- 2026-10-10 — refresh under approval. Copied: system-requirements.toml (re-attested: SR-020, SR-046); low-level-requirements.toml (re-attested: LLR-047); test-cases.toml (re-attested: TC-047). Registries not named by this act keep their prior snapshot bytes."]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\acts.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":578,"oldLines":3,"newStart":578,"newLines":9,"lines":[" date = \"2026-10-10\""," approved = []"," reattested = [\"SR-019\", \"SR-020\", \"SR-032\", \"SR-046\"]","+","+[[act]]","+seq = 96","+date = \"2026-10-10\"","+approved = []","+reattested = [\"LLR-047\", \"SR-020\", \"SR-046\", \"TC-047\"]"]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\requirements\\low-level-requirements.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":501,"oldLines":7,"newStart":501,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"Run capability menu reader\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py;project-trajectory/scripts/run.template.sh;project-trajectory/scripts/run.template.cmd;project-trajectory/scripts/run.template.command\""," code_symbol = \"load_capabilities/interactive_menu/direct/launch/_interpreter_dir/write_interpreter_shims\"","-detail = \"load_capabilities parses the docs/stack.ini [run] section (configparser, interpolation=None, case-preserving optionxform; declaration order kept; a name.desc row attaches to its command and an orphan or empty-value command is dropped) into an ordered (name, command, desc) list; --list prints name<TAB>desc per row; direct(name) launches by exact-match name (exit 2 on an unknown name) and launch() runs the shell line via subprocess shell=True, passing the child exit code straight through. launch puts first on PATH a directory whose bare python runs the menu interpreter exactly on every platform: on Windows that interpreter's own directory, and on POSIX a per-launch temporary directory of executable shims that exec its exact path. On POSIX that directory also makes bare python3 exact; a line intended to run on Windows declares python. The POSIX construction is a shim rather than a link so a virtual environment retains its configuration. interactive_menu prints the numbered menu, reads a pick from stdin, and returns EOF-safe without hanging; an absent or empty [run] prints guidance and exits 1. Every run launcher form resolves one interpreter by running, in order, its repository virtual-environment candidates and then the candidates from its dev-setup's ordinary runtime search, taking the first that reports Python 3.11 or later as its executable path; it runs the menu on exactly that path. The no-argument launchers change to the repository root and invoke dev-setup's --for-run/-ForRun readiness operation once before the menu, handing it that interpreter when one resolves; a nonzero result prints guidance and leaves the menu unopened. Direct and --list forms bypass that operation and, on Windows, the closing pause, and exit 1 with guidance when no interpreter resolves; run.command delegates to run.sh so it does not duplicate the operation, and non-interactive menu input remains available after it.\"","+detail = \"load_capabilities parses the docs/stack.ini [run] section (configparser, interpolation=None, case-preserving optionxform; declaration order kept; a name.desc row attaches to its command and an orphan or empty-value command is dropped) into an ordered (name, command, desc) list; --list prints name<TAB>desc per row; direct(name) launches by exact-match name (exit 2 on an unknown name) and launch() runs the shell line via subprocess shell=True, passing the child exit code straight through. launch prepends to PATH a directory whose bare python resolves to the menu interpreter: on Windows that interpreter's own directory, and on POSIX a per-launch temporary directory of executable shims that exec its exact path. On Windows the command processor searches the line's working directory before PATH, so a python.exe there, whether at the repository root or in a directory the line enters, runs instead and is outside this guarantee; when that directory has no interpreter, the prepended directory governs PATH lookup. On POSIX that directory also makes bare python3 exact; a line intended to run on Windows declares python. The POSIX construction is a shim rather than a link so a virtual environment retains its configuration. interactive_menu prints the numbered menu, reads a pick from stdin, and returns EOF-safe without hanging; an absent or empty [run] prints guidance and exits 1. Every run launcher form resolves one interpreter by running, in order, its repository virtual-environment candidates and then the candidates from its dev-setup's ordinary runtime search, taking the first that reports Python 3.11 or later as its executable path; it runs the menu on exactly that path. The no-argument launchers change to the repository root and invoke dev-setup's --for-run/-ForRun readiness operation once before the menu, handing it that interpreter when one resolves; a nonzero result prints guidance and leaves the menu unopened. Direct and --list forms bypass that operation and, on Windows, the closing pause, and exit 1 with guidance when no interpreter resolves; run.command delegates to run.sh so it does not duplicate the operation, and non-interactive menu input remains available after it.\""," rationale = \"\"\"The command being run is the user's own declared line, so shell=True is the same trust boundary the declaration already establishes rather than a new one. EOF-safety is load-bearing: a menu that blocks on a closed stdin turns an unattended invocation into a hang, which is the failure this launcher exists to avoid.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-047; TC-342)\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\requirements\\system-requirements.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":130,"oldLines":7,"newStart":130,"newLines":7,"lines":[" boundary_refs = [\"B-01\", \"B-04\"]"," hat_refs = [\"DATA-PROTECTION\", \"SECURITY\"]"," requirement = \"\"\"The pre-push hook shall scan the outgoing commit range and block a push that publishes a secret or (under privacy-check) a private identity.\"\"\"","-rationale = \"\"\"Realizes SN-005 and SN-009 — the last gate before content leaves the machine. Parents the interpreter probe, with SR-019: LLR-021 is declared on this hook's module, and a pre-push hook that cannot start scans nothing. The probe is mechanism, not a second obligation.\"\"\"","+rationale = \"\"\"Realizes SN-005 and SN-009 — the last gate before content leaves the machine. Parents the interpreter probe, with SR-019: pre-push sources the shared probe declared by LLR-021, and a pre-push hook that cannot start scans nothing. The probe is mechanism, not a second obligation.\"\"\""," acceptance_criteria = \"The hook scans the push range and blocks when a secret (always) or an identity/PII class (privacy-check on) appears. With no interpreter at the runtime floor, it refuses the push, names the developer-setup install step, and neither scans below that floor nor skips the scan.\""," priority = \"M\""," verification = \"Test\""]},{"oldStart":328,"oldLines":7,"newStart":328,"newLines":7,"lines":[" hat_refs = [\"CONSISTENCY\", \"CROSS-PLATFORM\"]"," requirement = \"\"\"The launchers at the repository root shall expose every declared runnable capability consistently to an interactive operator, to a direct caller naming one capability, and to automated discovery - from a single declaration, on Windows and POSIX alike.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"Realizes SN-001 - the evaluator's rungs make running the product never depend on recalling a command, and the capability set is declared once in docs/stack.ini rather than hard-wired and duplicated across the platform launchers. ONE OBLIGATION, not a menu specification: the row states the one capability-level decision - every declared capability is reachable, the same way, from one declaration, by three kinds of caller - because numbering, listing format, declaration grammar, fallback text and exit handling fail independently, which makes an enumeration of them a decomposition wearing a requirement's clothes. Those are LLR-047's and the acceptance's.\"\"\"","-acceptance_criteria = \"A machine listing prints one name-and-description line per declared capability in declaration order; a direct call naming a declared capability runs that capability's command and returns its exit code; the no-arg menu launches a stdin-selected capability non-interactively and does not hang on closed stdin; an absent or empty capability declaration prints guidance and exits 1; a declared description renders in both the menu and the listing; the platform launchers delegate to the one selector rather than carrying commands of their own; a fresh scaffold ships the selector and the delegate launchers. A no-argument launch runs its readiness step once from the repository root before the menu and, if the runtime remains missing, exits with guidance; a direct or discovery launch skips that step and any closing pause; piped menu selection survives the readiness step; and a declared command using bare python to invoke the language runtime resolves to the floor-resolved interpreter on every supported platform, while a declared command using bare python3 resolves to it on POSIX.\"","+acceptance_criteria = \"A machine listing prints one name-and-description line per declared capability in declaration order; a direct call naming a declared capability runs that capability's command and returns its exit code; the no-arg menu launches a stdin-selected capability non-interactively and does not hang on closed stdin; an absent or empty capability declaration prints guidance and exits 1; a declared description renders in both the menu and the listing; the platform launchers delegate to the one selector rather than carrying commands of their own; a fresh scaffold ships the selector and the delegate launchers. A no-argument launch runs its readiness step once from the repository root before the menu and, if the runtime remains missing, exits with guidance; a direct or discovery launch skips that step and any closing pause; piped menu selection survives the readiness step; and a declared command using bare python to invoke the language runtime resolves to the floor-resolved interpreter on every supported platform except that, on Windows, a python.exe in the line's working directory takes precedence, so the guarantee there applies when that directory contains no python.exe; a declared command using bare python3 resolves to it on POSIX.\""," priority = \"S\""," verification = \"Test\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\test\\test-cases.toml","hunks":[{"oldStart":504,"oldLines":7,"newStart":504,"newLines":7,"lines":[" level = \"Integration\""," method = \"Run the run-menu suite: --list prints name<TAB>desc for each declared capability in declaration order (an empty desc renders as a trailing tab); run_menu.py <name> runs the command and returns its exit code (a nonzero passes through); the no-arg menu launches a capability from piped stdin and returns without hanging on closed stdin; an unknown name exits 2 and an absent or empty [run] section prints guidance and exits 1; a name.desc row renders in the menu; and the scaffold ships scripts/run_menu.py plus run.* launchers that delegate to it with no RUN_CMD. With a real pre-3.11 interpreter first on PATH, run a declared bare-python capability on every platform; on POSIX also put another python beside the menu interpreter and drive the written bare-python and bare-python3 shims.\""," tier = \"Full\"","-expected = \"Satisfies SR-046 AcceptanceCriteria. A declared bare-python capability runs on the menu interpreter rather than the first PATH interpreter; on POSIX, bare-python and bare-python3 remain exact even where the menu interpreter's directory has another python, and each shim preserves the named virtual environment.\"","+expected = \"Satisfies SR-046 AcceptanceCriteria. A declared bare-python capability runs on the menu interpreter rather than the first PATH interpreter when its Windows working directory contains no python.exe; a python.exe in that directory precedes PATH on Windows and is outside this case. On POSIX, bare-python and bare-python3 remain exact even where the menu interpreter's directory has another python, and each shim preserves the named virtual environment.\""," automated = \"Yes\""," evidence = \"tests/test_run_menu.py::test_list_is_stable_name_tab_desc; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_direct_launch_runs_command_and_passes_args; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_direct_launch_passes_exit_code; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_unknown_name_exits_2; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_menu_launches_from_piped_stdin; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_menu_does_not_hang_on_closed_stdin; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_absent_run_section_prints_guidance_and_exits_1; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_a_capabilitys_python_is_the_menus_own_interpreter; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_a_capabilitys_python_is_exact_where_its_directory_holds_another; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_the_posix_shim_runs_exactly_the_interpreter_it_names[python]; tests/test_run_menu.py::test_the_posix_shim_runs_exactly_the_interpreter_it_names[python3]; tests/test_bootstrap.py::test_run_launchers_delegate_to_run_menu\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-880\\doc